course: ai
lesson: 19
title: Counting the AI in one ordinary day
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most AI is not a talking robot; it is small and quiet, hidden inside things you use without thinking.
explain: Try Mia's family game: from breakfast to bedtime, count every time a machine guessed something for you. The phone camera, the map, the speaker, the spam filter, the bank's fraud check, the weather forecast: they found 14 in one day.
explain: Each helper is good at one job and blind to everything else: the speaker will play "the poop song" a forty-seventh time without ever getting bored, because it has no idea when enough is enough.
explain: Noticing these helpers calmly, and checking them where it matters, is the grown-up way to live with them.
fact: A smart speaker never gets bored; it will play the same song as often as it is asked, so grown-ups may set limits.
fact: Spam filters use AI to spot junk and scam emails, but some real emails end up in the spam folder too.
fact: Banks use AI to spot unusual spending and block possible fraud; sometimes a real payment is blocked by mistake.
fact: To check that a message really comes from the bank, use the bank's own app or known number, not a link in the message.
fact: Weather forecasts use computer models and AI to make good guesses, but weather is hard to predict, so forecasts are not promises.
fact: Some farm tractors steer themselves using GPS (signals from satellites that tell a machine exactly where it is), and farms use AI to help plant, spray and check crops.
fact: Computer players in games are AI opponents, usually with difficulty levels that people set.
fact: Some apps identify birds from their songs by matching sounds to recordings they learned from; they can guess the wrong bird.
fact: Some hearing aids use AI to make voices clearer in noisy rooms.
fact: Adverts usually follow what you searched and watched online; it is not proven that phones secretly listen to talk to choose adverts, though many people wonder.
fact: AI is already part of everyday life in many small ways, and noticing it calmly is wiser than ignoring it or fearing all of it.
exercise: Maddie's little brother asks the speaker for "the poop song" 47 times. Why does it keep playing? | Machines don't know when enough is enough | It loves the song just as much as he does | It is trying to teach him a lesson | It hopes he will get bored first
exercise: What can the family do about the poop song? | A grown-up can set limits on the speaker | Wait until the speaker gets tired of it on its own | Ask the speaker nicely to feel sorry for them | Teach the speaker a nicer song to play instead
exercise: Jake's spam folder catches a scam email. How does the filter decide? | It uses AI to spot signs of junk and scams | A person reads every email before Jake does | It deletes every single email that comes from strangers | It only checks emails that have pictures in them
exercise: Why should grown-ups sometimes look inside the spam folder? | Real emails can land there by mistake | Spam emails turn into real emails after a week | The filter needs to be thanked now and then | Scam emails are safe once they are in spam
exercise: Mia's mum gets a text from her bank about a blocked payment. How did the bank notice it? | AI spotted spending that looked unusual | A bank worker follows her around the shops | The shop phoned the bank to complain about her | The payment was far too small to be a real one
exercise: How should Mia's mum check that the text is really from the bank? | Use the bank's own app or known phone number | Click the link in the text and log in | Reply to the text with her password | Call the phone number written at the bottom of the text
exercise: The forecast said sunny for Sam's party, and it rained. What does this show? | Forecasts are good guesses, not promises | The forecast lied on purpose to spoil the party | Forecasts are always wrong about rain in summer | The weather changed just because of the party
exercise: What is the wise plan for Sam's next outdoor party? | Have a rainy-day plan ready too | Ignore the forecast completely next time | Only ever have parties in winter | Ask the forecast to promise sunshine
exercise: On Lily's farm trip a tractor steers itself. What helps it? | GPS and AI that guide it along the field | A horse walking in front of it | Remote control from the farmer's kitchen | Lines painted on the soil for it to follow
exercise: Connor realises his brilliant opponent is a computer player. What is it? | An AI opponent set to a difficulty level | A real champion hiding behind a name | A friend using a secret cheat code | A recording of last week's game
exercise: Chloe's app names a bird from its song. How? | It matches the sound to recordings it learned from | It sees the bird through the walls | It asks the other birds nearby what the singer is called | It guesses the most common bird every time
exercise: The bird app might be wrong. What should Chloe do? | Use the app, then check in a bird book | Believe the app over her own eyes | Stop listening to birds altogether | Only listen to the loudest birds
exercise: How does Maddie's grandma's hearing aid help her? | It uses AI to make voices clearer in noisy rooms | It translates every word into sign language | It lets her hear people talking in other houses down the street | It records talk so she can listen later
exercise: Jake gets toy adverts just after talking about toys. What is the usual explanation? | Adverts follow what he searched and watched | The phone records every word he says | The toy shop sent someone to listen | The adverts are chosen completely at random
exercise: Mia's family counts 14 bits of AI in one day. What is the lesson? | AI is already part of everyday life in small ways | Their house must be full of robots | They should switch off all 14 at once and never use them again | Most families use no AI at all
test: "this forecast is useless, said sun and we got soaked" Which is fair? | Forecasts are good guesses; weather is hard to predict | Forecasts are made up by bored people | Forecasts are always right if you read them twice and slowly | The forecast was right, the rain was fake
test: What would happen if a spam filter blocked every email from someone new? | Real emails from new people would be lost | All scams would stop for ever | The filter would become perfect | Nobody would ever notice any change
test: "my phone's def listening, i said trampoline and now its all trampoline ads" Best reply? | More often it's from what you searched or watched; check privacy settings | Yes, every phone records all the talk in the house all day and sends it off | No, ads are always chosen at random | Say a different word to break the phone
test: Your mate says, "I only use AI when I talk to a chatbot." What would Mia's family say? | AI hides in cameras, maps, filters and more, not just chatbots | Your mate is right, chatbots are the only AI anyone ever uses at all | AI is only used in factories and labs | AI is only used by grown-ups at work
test: In a cartoon the smart speaker groans, "Not the poop song AGAIN!" What does the cartoon get wrong? | Speakers never get bored, they just play it | Speakers only groan at songs about food | Nothing, speakers really hate that song | The speaker should have cried instead
test: A text says it is the bank and asks you to tap a link "to unblock your card". What is wise? | Check through the bank's own app, not the link | Tap the link quickly before it runs out and the card stays blocked | Reply with the card number to confirm | Forward it to friends to warn them
test: Connor beats the computer on "easy" and boasts he is the best player in the world. What should he remember? | The difficulty was set low; a computer player is not the world's best player | Beating any computer at all makes you the world champion of that game | Easy mode means the computer was really trying | Computers always lose to children on purpose
test: The bird app says "owl" in the middle of a sunny afternoon in a busy town. What should Chloe think? | It might be wrong, so check in a bird book | It must be right, apps cannot mistake birds | Owls only sing when apps are listening | The owl must have followed the phone
test: The bank blocked Dad's real payment for a holiday. What is the fair way to see it? | The fraud check makes mistakes sometimes, but it also stops real fraud | The bank wants to stop Dad having a holiday | The fraud check makes too many mistakes, so it should be switched off for ever | The holiday must be a scam if it was blocked
test: Mia's family found 14 bits of AI in one day. If each of 3 days had 14, how many would they count? | 42 | 17 | 28 | 34
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the presence of artificial intelligence in ordinary domestic life? | It is present in numerous small devices and services, frequently unnoticed | It is confined to conversational programs and humanoid robots | It is absent from households and present only in industry | It is present solely in devices of the most recent manufacture
exam: It is given that a meteorological forecast predicted sunshine and that rain subsequently fell. Identify the conclusion most consistent with the established facts. | Forecasts constitute well-informed estimates rather than guarantees | Forecasts are composed without reference to any data | The forecast was deliberately falsified by its authors | The rainfall must be attributed to an error in the calendar
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning advertisements displayed shortly after a subject has been discussed aloud. | They ordinarily derive from searches and viewing activity rather than from proven covert listening | They are invariably the result of the continuous recording of all conversations within the home | They are selected without any reference to the user whatsoever | They are chosen by employees present in the household
exam: In respect of a text message purporting to originate from a bank and concerning a blocked payment, which course of action is most prudent? | Verification by means of the bank's own application or known telephone number | Immediate use of the hyperlink contained in the message | Transmission of the card number in reply to the message | Disregard of all communications from any bank
